<div class="container pagehero__inner"><span class="eyebrow">⚜ Questions &amp; Customs ⚜</span>
<h1>Asked and Answered</h1>
<p>Everything the House gets asked about camp, in one place. If your question is not here, send a pigeon.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="section"><div class="container" style="max-width:820px">

  <div class="faq-group">
    <h2>Arriving &amp; staying</h2>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Which nights do we camp?</h3>
      <p>We claim the spot <b>Thursday, October 8</b>, and guildies camp from <b>Friday, October 9</b>.
        RV days run Thursday through Sunday.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>What does it cost?</h3>
      <p><b>Nothing for guildmates.</b> Bring what you can to help if you want to contribute —
        firewood, ice, a dish for the potluck. Nobody is counting.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>When should I turn up?</h3>
      <p><b>The earlier the better.</b> Camp goes up faster with hands on it, and the good light
        does not last.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Where do I park?</h3>
      <p><b>The regular lots, first come first served.</b> Nothing to arrange, and no pass to
        collect &mdash; just turn up and park. <b>Come early on the Friday</b>: the lots nearest
        the camping end of the park are the small ones and they go first.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">There is a <a href="/camp.html#parking">map of every lot in the
        park</a> on the camp page, with our site marked.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>How hot does it get?</h3>
      <p><b>Hot enough to plan around.</b> Our spot is pavement, and blacktop holds the heat long
        after the sun is off it. The year it bit us the air read 97&deg;F and it felt like 105 out
        there. Across the last twenty-six years that weekend has run anywhere from <b>78&deg;F to
        97&deg;F</b>, and last year it rained on the Friday &mdash; so come ready for both.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:8px"><b>Bring your own shade</b> &mdash; a canopy, a tarp, anything.
        It is the one thing that makes the difference. Bring more water than you think, too.
        <a href="/camp.html#parking">More on the camp page</a>.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Can I bring a tent?</h3>
      <p><b>Yes.</b> Pitch it at the site and you are welcome — though be warned it may not be the
        most comfortable night of your life. A bunk in the RV is the softer option if one is free.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Can I bring my own RV?</h3>
      <p><b>The House has one RV space, and our rig is in it.</b> There is no second spot to park
        beside us.</p>
      <p>If you want to bring your own, you would need to <b>reserve your own space directly with
        the festival</b> — that runs about <b>$980</b>. Worth knowing before you plan around it.</p>
      <p>The space we do have takes a Fifth Wheel, Bumper Pull or Pop-Up Trailer, and
        <b>trailers must be unhooked</b> — no vehicle may stay hitched during the event.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group">
    <h2>Facilities</h2>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Are there showers?</h3>
      <p><b>No showers on site.</b> There is no water service, so shower at home before you come.
        There were public showers in the faire grounds last year, which may be an option — worth
        checking when you arrive rather than counting on.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Where are the bathrooms?</h3>
      <p>A potty in the RV, plus porta-potties close by in our camp area.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Is there water or a dump station?</h3>
      <p>No. It is a <b>dry camp</b> — Clark County provides no water service, dump station or
        servicing at all. Plan accordingly.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group">
    <h2>Food, drink &amp; the fire</h2>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Can I put things in the refrigerator?</h3>
      <p>Yes — help yourself. <b>Beers, snacks, whatever you like.</b> There will already be a few
        cold ones in there and some food at camp.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>What about drink?</h3>
      <p><b>Bring your own.</b> The House keeps a few beers in the fridge, but past that it is
        every guildmate to their own bottle.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Is there a potluck?</h3>
      <p>There is. Bring a dish to share if you can, and eat as you will — in the faire or by
        the fire.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Can we have a fire?</h3>
      <p>Yes. <b>We will have a firepit with a lid.</b> Bring firewood if you like — <b>small logs</b>,
        nothing you need an axe and an afternoon for.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group">
    <h2>Bunks &amp; wristbands</h2>

    <div class="faq"><h3>How do I get a bunk?</h3>
      <p>Claim one under <a href="/members#bunks">Profile</a>. The main bunk is Mama Bear’s and another is Sir Captain Caberk’s — those two are theirs for the
        whole weekend and are not up for grabs.</p>
      <p>That leaves <b>three open bunks each night</b>, nine across the weekend, and they are kept
        for guildmates who have <b>already camped a faire</b> with the House. Beyond that it is
        first come, first served, and you may hold <b>one bed per night</b>.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Is a bunk an actual bed?</h3>
      <p>Two of the three are beds in the rig. <b>Bunk 3 has a strong chance of being a cot</b>
        rather than a bed &mdash; worth knowing before you claim that one. It is marked on every
        bunk board on the site.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Is bedding provided?</h3>
      <p><b>No — bring your own.</b> A sleeping bag and your pillow are the way. And yes, maybe a
        teddy bear.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>How do wristbands work?</h3>
      <p><b>Every bunk comes with a wristband</b>, and that band <b>includes your entry to the faire</b>
        from the camping area. Show it at the <b>RV campsite gate</b> and the guards will wave you
        through. No wristband, no entry past the gate.</p>
      <p><b>So do not buy a ticket if you have a bunk.</b> The band already covers you — there is no
        need to pay twice, and people have.</p>
      <p><b>Wear it loose.</b> If you are only staying a short while, slip it off when you go and
        hand it to the guildmate taking your bunk next — one band, passed along, and their day is
        covered too.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>What if my plans change?</h3>
      <p>Release your bunk under Profile so a guildmate can take it. If anyone is waiting, it
        passes to them straight away and they are told.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group">
    <h2>Customs of the House</h2>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Can I bring my dog?</h3>
      <p><b>No animals.</b> That is the faire’s rule, not ours — no pets, and no emotional
        support animals either. Only <b>service animals under the Americans with Disabilities
        Act</b> are let through, and they must stay under their handler’s control.</p>
      <p class="muted" style="font-size:.9rem">The gate does enforce it. The full wording is on
        the <a href="/camp.html#faire" style="color:var(--wine);font-weight:600">Ren Faire Camp</a> page.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Do I have to be in garb?</h3>
      <p>Everyone does their own. Wear what you have, wear what you have made, wear what you
        found in a bin at the back of a shop. No House uniform.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Who do I ask about anything else?</h3>
      <p>Send word to <b>Mama Bear</b> — <a href="/pigeon.html">send a pigeon</a> and she will
        answer.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-group">
    <h2>The tavern &amp; your details</h2>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Who can see what I write?</h3>
      <p>The Tavern is public — anyone may read it, signed in or not. You need a seat or a Guild
        Hall account to post. You can edit or remove your own words at any time; the Guild Leader
        can strike anything that needs striking.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>Who can see my contact details?</h3>
      <p>Your contact email and phone number are visible to <b>the Guild Leader only</b> — they are
        there so she can reach you about camp, and nowhere else on the site. Your name, face and
        rank appear on the public guild roster.</p></div>

    <div class="faq"><h3>What is a Pledge?</h3>
      <p>Anyone can make an account, and everyone starts as a <b>Pledge</b>. Pledges can talk in the
        Tavern, take a card each night and claim off the bring-list.</p>
      <p><b>Becoming a guildmate is not automatic.</b> The Guild Leader considers pledges one at a
        time — the House is small, the camp smaller, and not everyone who signs up will be taken in.
        Nothing to apply for and nobody to chase; be about the place and she will know you.</p>
      <p>Bunks come later still, once you have camped a faire with us.</p></div>
  </div>

  <p class="muted center" style="margin-top:34px">
